em from multiple angles and confirms the conclusion through different reasoning paths. All approaches consistently lead to the same result, reinforcing the validity of the solution.", "deepseek_solution": "Given the equations:\n\n1. \\( x + y + z = a \\)\n2. \\( \\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{y} + \\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{1}{a} \\)\n\n**Step-by-Step Solution:**\n\n1. **Express the reciprocal equation with a common denominator:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{xy + yz + zx}{xyz} = \\frac{1}{a} \\implies xy + yz + zx = \\frac{xyz}{a}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Relate symmetric sums:** Let \\( S = x + y + z = a \\), \\( Q = xy + yz + zx \\), and \\( P = xyz \\). From the reciprocal equation:\n   \\[\n   Q = \\frac{P}{a}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Form the cubic polynomial with roots \\( x, y, z \\):**\n   \\[\n   t^3 - St^2 + Qt - P = 0 \\implies t^3 - at^2 + \\left(\\frac{P}{a}\\right)t - P = 0\n   \\]\n\n4. **Factor the cubic polynomial by testing \\( t = a \\):**\n   Substituting \\( t = a \\):\n   \\[\n   a^3 - a^3 + \\frac{P}{a} \\cdot a - P = 0 \\implies 0 = 0\n   \\]\n   Thus, \\( t = a \\) is a root, and the polynomial factors as:\n   \\[\n   (t - a)(t^2 + bt + c) = 0\n   \\]\n   Matching coefficients confirms \\( b = 0 \\) and \\( c = \\frac{P}{a} \\), leading to:\n   \\[\n   (t - a)(t^2 + \\frac{P}{a}) = 0\n   \\]\n\n5. **Determine the roots:**\n   The roots are \\( t = a \\), \\( t = \\sqrt{-\\frac{P}{a}} \\), and \\( t = -\\sqrt{-\\frac{P}{a}} \\). This explicitly shows one root is \\( a \\), and the others are additive inverses.\n\n6. **Contradiction approach (alternative method):**\n   Assume \\( x, y, z \\neq a \\). Substitute \\( z = a - x - y \\) into the reciprocal equation and simplify. This leads to contradictions \\( (x - a)(y - a) = 0 \\), forcing \\( x = a \\) or \\( y = a \\), contradicting the assumption.\n\n**Conclusion:** One of \\( x, y, z \\) must equal \\( a \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{One of } x, y, z \\text{ is equal to } a}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. We start with the given equations:\n   \\[\n   x + y + z = a\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{y} + \\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{1}{a}\n   \\]\n\n2. From the second equation, express it in terms of a single fraction:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{y z + x z + x y}{x y z} = \\frac{1}{a}\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify the fraction by multiplying both sides by \\( x y z \\):\n   \\[\n   y z + x z + x y = \\frac{x y z}{a}\n   \\]\n\n4. Substitute \\( x + y + z = a \\) and multiply through by \\( a \\):\n   \\[\n   a(yz + xz + xy) = xyz\n   \\]\n\n5. Rearrange the terms:\n   \\[\n   xyz = a(yz + xz + xy)\n   \\]\n\n6. Isolate the terms involving \\( x, y, z \\):\n   \\[\n   xyz - a(yz + xz + xy) = 0\n   \\]\n\n7. Further simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   xyz = a(yz + xz + xy)\n   \\]\n\n8. Divide both sides by \\( yz \\):\n   \\[\n   x = a \\left( 1 + \\frac{x}{y} + \\frac{x}{z} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n   Realizing there's an easier avenue, inspect the earlier simplification:\n\n9. We return to the detailed calculation approach and expand left:\n   \\[\n   yz + xz + xy = \\frac{xyz}{a}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x^2 z + x^2 y + y^2 z + 2 xyz + xy^2 + y z^2 + x z^2 = 0 \n   \\]\n\n10. Group the terms:\n    \\[\n    x^2(y+z) + y^2(z) + 2xyz + z^2y + x^2(z) = 0 \n    \\]\n\n11. Factor out common terms:\n    \\[\n    (y+z)(x^2+yz+xy+xz) = 0 \n    \\]\n    \\[\n    (y+z)[x(x+y)+z(x+y)] = 0 \n    \\]\n\n12. Factor further:\n    \\[\n    (y+z)(x+y)(x+z) = 0 \n    \\]\n\n13. Since for any product to be zero, at least one of the factors must be zero, combine the conditions:\n    \\[\n    (a-x)(a-y)(a-z) = 0\n    \\]\n\n14. Therefore, for the entire product to be zero, at least one among \\( (a-x), (a-y) \\), or \\( (a-z) \\) must be zero. This implies:\n    \\[\n    \\boxed{x = a \\text{ or } y = a \\text{ or } z = a}\n    \\]\n\nHence, one of the variables \\( x, y, \\) or \\( z \\) must equal \\( a \\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In triangle \\( \\t