he position of point \\( Q \\) on \\( BC \\):**\n   - Since \\( \\triangle QAC \\sim \\triangle ABC \\), the corresponding sides are proportional:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{QC}{AC} = \\frac{AC}{BC} = \\frac{QA}{AB}\n     \\]\n   - Substituting the known lengths:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{QC}{6} = \\frac{6}{11} \\implies QC = \\frac{36}{11}\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, \\( BQ = BC - QC = 11 - \\frac{36}{11} = \\frac{85}{11} \\).\n\n3. **Calculating the length of \\( PQ \\):**\n   - Points \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) are on \\( BC \\), so the distance \\( PQ \\) is the absolute difference between their positions along \\( BC \\):\n     \\[\n     PQ = \\left| BQ - BP \\right| = \\left| \\frac{85}{11} - \\frac{64}{11} \\right| = \\frac{21}{11}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the length of \\( PQ \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{21}{11}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Identify the Similar Triangles:**\n   Given that $\\triangle P B A$ is similar to $\\triangle A B C$ and $\\triangle Q A C$ is similar to $\\triangle A B C$, we can utilize the properties of similar triangles.\n\n2. **Set Up the Ratios:**\n   From the similarity of $\\triangle P B A$ and $\\triangle A B C$, the corresponding sides are proportional. Hence, we use the ratio of the sides:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{P B}{A B} = \\frac{A B}{C B}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the given lengths:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{P B}{8} = \\frac{8}{11}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve for $P B$:**\n   Cross-multiplying to solve for $PB$:\n   \\[\n   P B = \\frac{8 \\times 8}{11} = \\frac{64}{11}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Set Up the Ratios for $\\triangle Q A C$:**\n   Similarly, from the similarity of $\\triangle Q A C$ and $\\triangle A B C$, the corresponding sides are proportional. Hence, we use the ratio of the sides:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{Q C}{A C} = \\frac{A C}{B C}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the given lengths:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{Q C}{6} = \\frac{6}{11}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Solve for $Q C$:**\n   Cross-multiplying to solve for $QC$:\n   \\[\n   Q C = \\frac{6 \\times 6}{11} = \\frac{36