(BC\\) at \\(M\\) and the extensions of \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) at \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is the A-excircle.\n2. The tangents from \\(A\\) to the A-excircle are equal, so \\(AP = AQ\\).\n3. Let \\(AP = AQ = x\\). The tangents from \\(B\\) to the A-excircle are \\(BP = BM = y\\), and from \\(C\\) are \\(CQ = CM = z\\).\n4. Using the semiperimeter \\(p = \\frac{AB + BC + AC}{2}\\), we express \\(AP\\) in terms of the sides:\n   \\[\n   AP = AB + BP = AB + y\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   AQ = AC + CQ = AC + z\n   \\]\n5. Since \\(AP = AQ\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   AB + y = AC + z\n   \\]\n6. Substituting \\(BC = y + z\\) into the equation, we solve for \\(y\\) and \\(z\\):\n   \\[\n   AB + y = AC + (BC - y)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   AB + 2y = AC + BC\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{AC + BC - AB}{2}\n   \\]\n7. Substituting \\(y\\) back into \\(AP\\):\n   \\[\n   AP = AB + \\frac{AC + BC - AB}{2} = \\frac{AB + AC + BC}{2} = p\n   \\]\nThus, \\(AP = p\\).\n\n### Part b: Prove that \\(BM = CK\\).\n\n1. The incircle touches \\(BC\\) at \\(K\\). The tangent lengths from \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) to the incircle are:\n   \\[\n   BK = \\frac{AB + BC - AC}{2}, \\quad CK = \\frac{AC + BC - AB}{2}\n   \\]\n2. The A-excircle touches \\(BC\\) at \\(M\\). The tangent lengths from \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) to the A-excircle are:\n   \\[\n   BM = \\frac{AB + BC - AC}{2}, \\quad CM = \\frac{AC + BC - AB}{2}\n   \\]\n3. Since \\(CK = \\frac{AC + BC - AB}{2}\\) and \\(BM = \\frac{AB + BC - AC}{2}\\), we compare:\n   \\[\n   BM = \\frac{AB + BC - AC}{2}, \\quad CK = \\frac{AC + BC - AB}{2}\n   \\]\n4. However, this is incorrect. Correctly, from part a, \\(BM = p - AC\\) and \\(CK = p - AB\\). Since \\(p = \\frac{AB + BC + AC}{2}\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   BM = p - AC = \\frac{AB + BC + AC}{2} - AC = \\frac{AB + BC - AC}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   CK = p - AB = \\frac{AB + BC + AC}{2} - AB = \\frac{AC + BC - AB}{2}\n   \\]\n5. Therefore, \\(BM = CK\\).\n\n### Part c: Prove that \\(BC = PL\\).\n\n1. \\(P\\) is on the extension of \\(AB\\) beyond \\(B\\), and \\(L\\) is the point where the incircle touches \\(AB\\).\n2. The distance \\(PL\\) is the sum of \\(BP\\) and \\(BL\\):\n   \\[\n   PL = BP + BL\n   \\]\n3. From part a, \\(BP = p - AB = \\frac{AB + BC + AC}{2} - AB = \\frac{BC + AC - AB}{2}\\).\n4. The incircle tangent length from \\(A\\) to \\(L\\) is \\(AL = \\frac{AB + AC - BC}{2}\\). Thus, \\(BL = AB - AL = \\frac{AB + BC - AC}{2}\\).\n5. Adding these distances:\n   \\[\n   PL = \\frac{BC + AC - AB}{2} + \\frac{AB + BC - AC}{2} = \\frac{2BC}{2} = BC\n   \\]\nThus, \\(BC = PL\\).\n\n### Final Answers\n\na) \\(\\boxed{AP = p}\\)\n\nb) \\(\\boxed{BM = CK}\\)\n\nc) \\(\\boxed{BC = PL}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven:\n- A circle touches side $BC$ of triangle $ABC$ at point $M$ and extensions of sides $AB$ and $AC$ at points $P$ and $Q$, respectively.\n- An inscribed circle of $\\triangle ABC$ touches side $BC$ at point $K$ and side $AB$ at point $L$.\n- We need to prove:\n    (a) $A P$ is equal to the semiperimeter $p$ of $\\triangle ABC$.\n    (b) $BM = CK$.\n    (c) $BC = PL$.\n\n#### Part (a): Prove that $AP = p$\n\n1. Consider two tangent segments drawn from the same external point to a circle. These segments are equal. So, for triangle $ABC$:\n\n    - From point $A$, $AP = AQ$ (since $P$ and $Q$ are tangent points from $A$)\n    - From point $B$, $BM = BP$ (tangent segments from $B$)\n    - From point $C$, $CM = CQ$ (tangent segments from $C$)\n\n2. The perimeter of triangle $ABC$ can be written in terms of the tangent segments:\n\n    \\[\n    AB + BC + AC = AB + (BM + MC) + AC\n    \\]\n\n3. Substitute equal tangent segments:\n\n    \\[\n    AB + BC + AC = AB + (BP + CQ) + AC = AB + BP + CQ + AC = (AP - AB + AQ - AC) + AB + AC\n    \\]\n\n4. Rearrange and combine like terms:\n\n    \\[\n    AB + BC + AC = (AB + AC) + (BP + CQ) = 2AP\n    \\]\n\n5. Hence,\n\n    \\[\n    2AP = AB + BC + AC \\implies AP = \\frac{AB + BC + AC}{2}\n    \\]\n\nThus, $AP$ is equal to the semiperimeter $p$ of $\\triangle ABC$.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP = p}\n\\]\n\n#### Part (b): Prove that $BM = CK$\n\n1. From the given problem, we have established that from point $B$, $BM = BP$, and from point $A$, we know:\n\n    \\[\n    BP = AP - AB\n    \\]\n\n2. Similarly, from point $C$, using the inscribed circle:\n\n    \\[\n    CK = p - AC\n    \\]\n\n3. Given $AP = p$, substitute into the BP expression:\n\n    \\[\n    BM = BP = AP - AB = p - AB\n    \\]\n\n4. By definition of the semiperimeter:\n\n    \\[\n    CK = p - AC\n    \\]\n\nTherefore, $BM = CK$ as they both are equal to $p - AB$ and $p - AC$ respectively.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{BM = CK}\n\\]\n\n#### Part (c): Prove that $BC = PL$\n\n1. When dealing with the two tangent segments from $L$, tangent to the inscribed circle of $\\triangle ABC$, we have:\n\n    \\[\n    PL = AL + BL\n    \\]\n\n2. We already know:\n\n    \\[\n    PL = p - AB\n    \\]\n\n3. To equate $PL$ with $BC$, observe that:\n\n    \\[\n    BC = AB + BL - (PL - BL)\n    \\]\n\n4. Given that $AP = p$, thus $PL = AP = p - AB$.\n\nTherefore, $PL$ must be equal to $BC$ because:\n\n    \\[\n    BC = p - AB + BL\n    \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{BC = PL}\n\\]\n\nThus, all the required conditions are proven.\n\\[\\blacksquare\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In a circle, a chord of length 10 cm is drawn. A tangent to the circle is drawn through one end of the chord, and a secant, parallel to the tangent, is drawn through the other end of the chord. The internal segment of the secant is equal to 12 cm. Find the radius of the circle.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the radius of a circle where there's a chord of length 10 cm. One end of the chord has a tangent, and the other end has a secant parallel to the tangent, with the internal segment of the secant being 12 cm. Hmm, let me visualize this.\n\nFirst, let me draw a circle. Let's say the c