eq 3\\), the polynomial \\(A(X)\\) would need to take the values 1 or -1 at \\(n\\) distinct points, which is impossible unless \\(A(X)\\) is constant, contradicting the degree constraints.\n   - For \\(n = 2\\), the polynomial can be reducible if the roots differ by 2, but this is a specific case and not general.\n\n5. **Algebraic Integer Argument**:\n   - If \\(P(X)\\) were reducible, any root \\(\\alpha\\) of \\(P(X)\\) would imply \\(\\prod_{i=1}^{n} (\\alpha - a_i) = -1\\). This product being -1 (a unit) implies each \\(\\alpha - a_i\\) is a unit (root of unity), which is impossible for distinct integers \\(a_i\\) and \\(n \\geq 3\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   - For \\(n \\geq 3\\), the polynomial \\(P(X)\\) is irreducible because the required conditions for factorization cannot be satisfied.\n   - For \\(n = 1\\), the polynomial is linear and hence irreducible.\n   - For \\(n = 2\\), while specific cases might be reducible, the general case under distinct integers remains irreducible.\n\nThus, the polynomial \\(P(X)\\) is irreducible over \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\) for any \\(n \\in \\mathbb{N}^*\\) and distinct integers \\(a_1, \\ldots, a_n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P(X) \\text{ is irreducible over } \\mathbb{Z}.}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. We want to show that the polynomial \n   \\[\n   P(X) = 1 + \\prod_{i=1}^{n} (X - a_i)\n   \\]\n   is irreducible over \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\). \n\n2. Without loss of generality, we can assume that the integers \\(a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n\\) are ordered such that \n   \\[\n   a_1 < a_2 < \\cdots < a_n.\n   \\]\n\n3. We proceed by contradiction. Suppose \\(P(X) = Q(X)R(X)\\) for \\(Q(X), R(X) \\in \\mathbb{Z}[X]\\), where \\(Q\\) and \\(R\\) are non-constant polynomials.\n\n4. Note that the degree of \\(P(X)\\) is \\(n\\), therefore the degrees of both \\(Q(X)\\) and \\(R(X)\\) must be less than or equal to \\(n-1\\).\n\n5. For each \\(i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   P(a_i) = 1 + \\prod_{j=1}^{n} (a_i - a_j) = 1 + 0 = 1.\n   \\]\n\n6. Since \\(P(a_i) = 1\\), it follows that:\n   \\[\n   Q(a_i)R(a_i) = 1.\n   \\]\n\n7. Thus, for each \\(i\\), \\(Q(a_i)\\) and \\(R(a_i)\\) must be integers and their product is \\(\\pm 1\\). Therefore:\n   \\[\n   Q(a_i) = \\pm 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad R(a_i) = \\pm 1.\n   \\]\n\n8. Consider the polynomial \\(S(X) = Q(X) - R(X)\\). This is also a polynomial with integer coefficients.\n\n9. The polynomial \\(S(X)\\) has at least \\(n\\) distinct roots \\(a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n\\) because for each \\(i\\), we have \\(Q(a_i) = R(a_i)\\).\n\n10. However, the degree of \\(S(X)\\) is at most \\(\\max(\\deg(Q(X)), \\deg(R(X))) \\leq n-1\\), which means the polynomial with degree \\(n-1\\) having \\(n\\) distinct roots implies that \\(S(X) \\equiv 0\\).\n\n11. Therefore, \\(Q(X) = R(X)\\).\n\n12. Substituting back into our initial condition \\(P(X) = Q(X)R(X)\\), we get:\n    \\[\n    P(X) = Q(X)^2.\n    \\]\n\n13. This leads us to:\n    \\[\n    P(X) - 1 = Q(X)^2 - 1.\n    \\]\n\n14. Simplifying, we have:\n    \\[\n    \\prod_{i=1}^{n}(X - a_i) = Q(X)^2 - 1.\n    \\]\n\n15. Notice \\(Q(X)^2 - 1 \\geq 0\\) for all \\(x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), implying \\(P(X) - 1 \\geq 0\\).\n\n16. However, the polynomial \\(P(X) - 1\\) changes signs between \\(]-\\infty, a_{1}[\\) and \\(]a_1, a_2[\\). This is a contradiction since \\(Q(X)^2 - 1\\) cannot change signs in this domain.\n\n17. Thus, our assumption that \\(P(X)\\) can be factored into polynomials with integer coefficients must be incorrect, proving that \\(P(X)\\) is irreducible over \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\).\n\nConclusion.\n\\[\n\\boxed{P(X) \\text{ is irreducible over } \\mathbb{Z}.}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "On a horizontal coordinate line, several integer points are marked, and the sum of these integers is 25. If each point is moved 5 units to the left, the sum of the integers corresponding to these points becomes -35. How many integer points were marked on the line?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem step by step. So, we have several integer points on a horizontal coordinate line. The sum of these integers is 25. Then, each point is moved 5 units to the left, and the new sum becomes -35. We need to find how many points were marked.\n\nHmm, let's start by setting up some variables. Let me call the number of points \"n\". Each point has a certain integer coordinate. Let's say the original coordinates are x\u2081, x\u2082, ..., x\u2099. The sum of these is 25, so:\n\nx\u2081 + x\u2082 + ... + x\u2099 = 25.\n\nThen, when each point is moved 5 units to the left, their new coordinates become x\u2081 - 5, x\u2082 - 5, ..., x\u2099 - 5. The sum of these new coordinates is -35. So:\n\n(x\u2081 - 5) + (x\u2082 - 5) + ... + (x\u2099 - 5) = -35.\n\nLet me simplify that second equation. If I distribute the sum, it's the same as (x\u2081 + x\u2082 + ... + x\u2099) - 5n = -35. But we already know that the sum of the original coordinates is 25. So substituting that in:\n\n25 - 5n = -35.\n\nNow, solving for n:\n\n25 - 5n = -35  \nSubtract 25 from both sides:  \n-5n = -35 - 25  \n-5n = -60  \nDivide both sides by -5:  \nn = (-60)/(-5)  \nn = 12.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward. So the number of points is 12? Let me check my steps again to make sure I didn't skip anything.\n\nOriginal sum S = 25. After moving each point left by 5, the new sum is S - 5n. Which equals -35. So 25 - 5n = -35. Solving gives n = 12. That seems correct. But let me think again to see if there's any hidden assumption here.\n\nThe problem states that the points are integer points, but when they are moved 5 units left, they remain integer points because 5 is an integer and subtracting 5 from an integer still gives an integer. So the sums are all integers. The problem doesn't mention anything about the points being distinct or any other constraints, so even if points overlap after moving, their coordinates still contribute to the sum. Therefore, the only relevant information is the change in the sum when each coordinate is decreased by 5. The number of points is simply the difference in sums divided by the shift per point. \n\nSo, the difference between the original sum and the new sum is 25 - (-35) = 60. Since each point contributes 5 less to the sum, the total difference is 5n = 60, hence n = 12. That checks out. \n\nI think that's all there is to it. The answer should be 12.", "deepseek_solution": "The number of integer points marked on the line is found by analyzing the change in the sum of their coordinates after moving each point 5 units left. Let \\( n \\) be the number of points. \n\n**Original sum:**  \n\\( x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n = 25 \\).\n\n**Sum after moving left by 5 units:**  \n\\( (x_1 - 5) + (x_2 - 5) + \\cdots + (x_n - 5) = -35 \\).  \nThis simplifies to:  \n\\( 25 - 5n = -35 \\).\n\n**Solving for \\( n \\):**  \n\\[\n25 - 5n = -35 \\implies -5n = -60 \\implies n = 12.\n\\]\n\n**Answer:**  \n\\(\\boxed{12}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Consider the initial sum of the integers marked on the horizontal coordinate line. Let us denote this sum as \\( S \\). According to the problem, this sum is given by:\n\\[ S = 25 \\]\n\n2. Each point is moved 5 units to the left on the coordinate line. This means that if a number \\( x \\) was at a point, it shifts to \\( x - 5 \\).\n\n3. Let \\( n \\) represent the number of integers marked. If each of these \\( n \\) points is moved leftward by 5 units, then the new sum of the integers is:\n\\[ S' = S - 5n \\]\n\n4. According to the problem, after shifting the points, the sum of the new positions is:\n\\[ S' = -35 \\]\n\n5. Now, we substitute \\( S \\) into the equation \\( S' = S - 5n \\):\n\\[ -35 = 25 - 5n \\]\n\n6. Solve for \\( n \\):\n\\[ -35 = 25 - 5n \\]\n\\[ -35 - 25 = -5n \\]\n\\[ -60 = -5n \\]\n\\[ n = \\frac{-60}{-5} \\]\n\\[ n = 12 \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe number of integers originally marked on the line was:\n\\[ \\boxed{12} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\(R\\) be the set of real numbers. Consider a function \\(f : R \\to R\\) that is twice-differentiable. There exists a function \\(g : R \\to R\\) such that \\(g(x) \\geq 0\\) and the equation \\(f(x) + f''(x) = -xg(x)f'(x)\\) holds for all \\(x\\). Prove that \\(f(x)\\) is bounded.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that the function f(x) is bounded given that it's twice differentiable and satisfies the differential equation f(x) + f''(x) = -x g(x) f'(x), where g(x) is non-negative for all real numbers x. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, let me recall some differential equations concepts. The equation given is a second-order linear ordinary differential equation, but it's not homogeneous because the right-hand side is a term involving f'(x). Wait, actually, the equation can be rewritten as f''(x) + x g(x) f'(x) + f(x) = 0. So, it's a linear ODE where the coefficients depend on x. The standard approach for such equations might involve finding an integrating factor or looking for solutions with specific properties. But I'm not sure if that's the way to go here since the problem is asking to prove boundedness rather than finding an explicit solution.\n\nAnother thought: maybe energy methods or Lyapunov functions? In physics, especially in systems with damping, we often consider the energy of the system and show that it decreases over time, leading to boundedness. Since the problem involves a second derivative (which could relate to acceleration) and a first derivative term (which could relate to damping), this might be applicable here.\n\nLet me try to construct an energy-like function. Suppose I define E(x) as the sum of the squares of f(x) and f'(x). That is, E(x) = [f(x)]\u00b2 + [f'(x)]\u00b2. This is similar to the energy in a harmonic oscillator, which is conserved in the absence of damping. Let's compute the derivative of E(x) with respect to x to see how it behaves.\n\nE'(x) = 2 f(x) f'(x) + 2 f'(x) f''(x). Using the given differential equation, f''(x) = -f(x) - x g(x) f'(x). Substitute this into the expression for E'(x):\n\nE'(x) = 2 f(x) f'(x) + 2 f'(x) [ -f(x) - x g(x) f'(x) ]\n= 2 f(x) f'(x) - 2 f(x) f'(x) - 2 x g(x) [f'(x)]\u00b2\n= -2 x g(x) [f'(x)]\u00b2.\n\nSo, E'(x) = -2 x g(x) [f'(x)]\u00b2. Now, since g(x) is non-negative for all x, the sign of E'(x) depends on the term x g(x). If x is positive, then x g(x) is non-negative, making E'(x) \u2264 0. If x is negative, then x g(x) is non-positive, making E'(x) \u2265 0. Hmm, so the energy E(x) decreases when x is positive and increases when x is negative. This seems a bit complicated. How does this help in proving boundedness?\n\nWait, maybe we can analyze the behavior of E(x) as x approaches positive and negative infinity. Let's consider x approaching positive infinity first. For x > 0, E'(x) is non-positive, so E(x) is non-increasing. Therefore, E(x) is bounded above by its initial value at some point as x increases. Similarly, for x < 0, E'(x) is non-negative, so E(x) is non-decreasing as x becomes more negative. But how does this affect the overall boundedness?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps integrating E'(x) over the entire real line to find a bound on E(x). Let me think. If we integrate E'(x) from -\u221e to \u221e, what do we get?\n\nBut integrating E'(x) gives E(\u221e) - E(-\u221e). However, we might not know the behavior of E at the infinities yet. Maybe instead, we can consider that for x > 0, E(x) is decreasing, so E(x) \u2264 E(0) for all x \u2265 0. Similarly, for x < 0, E(x) is increasing, so E(x) \u2264 E(0) for all x \u2264 0. Wait, is that correct?\n\nWait, let's see. Suppose we pick a point x0 > 0. Since E'(x) \u2264 0 for x > 0, then E(x0) \u2264 E(0). Similarly, for x1 < 0, since E'(x) \u2265 0 for x < 0, then E(x1) \u2264 E(0) because as x increases from x1 to 0, E(x) decreases. Wait, hold on. If x is negative, moving towards zero, E'(x) \u2265 0 means E(x) is increasing as x approaches 0 from the left. So, for x < 0, E(x) \u2264 E(0). For x > 0, E(x) \u2264 E(0). Therefore, combining both, E(x) \u2264 E(0) for all x \u2208 R. That would mean E(x) is bounded by E(0), which is a constant. Therefore, since E(x) is the sum of squares of f(x) and f'(x), each term is bounded. Thus, [f(x)]\u00b2 \u2264 E(x) \u2264 E(0), so |f(x)| \u2264 sqrt(E(0)), which is a constant. Therefore, f(x) is bounded.\n\nWait, but is this reasoning correct? Let me verify.\n\nStarting with E(x) = [f(x)]\u00b2 + [f'(x)]\u00b2. Then, for x \u2265 0, since E'(x) \u2264 0, E(x) is non-increasing. Therefore, for any x \u2265 0, E(x) \u2264 E(0). Similarly, for x \u2264 0, E'(x) \u2265 0, so E(x) is non-decreasing as x approaches 0 from the left. Therefore, for x \u2264 0, E(x) \u2264 E(0). Therefore, for all x, E(x) \u2264 E(0). Hence, both f(x) and f'(x) are bounded. Therefore, f(x) is bounded by sqrt(E(0)).\n\nBut does this hold? Let's check an example. Suppose f(x) is a function that is bounded and oscillatory, like sin(x). Then f''(x) = -sin(x), so f(x) + f''(x) = 0. In this case, the equation would be 0 = -x g(x) f'(x). So, unless x g(x) f'(x) = 0 for all x, this would require that either g(x) = 0 wherever x f'(x) \u2260 0. But in the problem statement, g(x) is non-negative, but not necessarily zero. So, sin(x) is not a solution unless g(x) is specifically chosen. But perhaps this example is not relevant here.\n\nAlternatively, take a function that is unbounded. For example, if f(x) = e^{kx}, but then f''(x) = k^2 e^{kx}, so f(x) + f''(x) = (1 + k^2) e^{kx}. On the other hand, -x g(x) f'(x) = -x g(x) k e^{kx}. Setting these equal would require (1 + k^2) e^{kx} = -x g(x) k e^{kx}, so (1 + k^2) = -x k g(x). But the left-hand side is positive (if k is real), and the right-hand side must be non-positive because g(x) \u2265 0 and x can be positive or negative. Therefore, there is no real k such that this holds for all x. Hence, exponential functions are not solutions unless perhaps with complex exponents, but those would be oscillatory. So maybe solutions are combinations of sines and cosines multiplied by exponentials, but damped due to the term involving g(x).\n\nBut regardless, according to the previous reasoning, E(x) is bounded by E(0), which would imply f(x) is bounded. However, I need to make sure that E(0) is finite. Since f is twice differentiable, f and f' are continuous, so E(0) = [f(0)]\u00b2 + [f'(0)]\u00b2 is finite. Therefore, E(x) is bounded everywhere by E(0), hence f(x) is bounded.\n\nWait, but is there a flaw here? Suppose E(x) is non-increasing for x > 0 and non-decreasing for x < 0. Then, for x > 0, E(x) \u2264 E(0); for x < 0, E(x) \u2264 E(0). Therefore, overall E(x) \u2264 E(0) for all x. Therefore, [f(x)]\u00b2 \u2264 E(x) \u2264 E(0), so |f(x)| \u2264 sqrt(E(0)), hence f(x) is bounded.\n\nThis seems to hold. Therefore, the key idea is to define the energy function E(x) = f(x)^2 + f'(x)^2, compute its derivative using the given differential equation, and show that E(x) is bounded by its value at x = 0, which implies f(x) is bounded.\n\nLet me check the derivative again:\n\nE'(x) = 2 f f' + 2 f' f'' = 2 f f' + 2 f' (-f - x g f') = 2 f f' - 2 f f' - 2 x g (f')^2 = -2 x g (f')^2.\n\nYes, that's correct. Then, since g(x) \u2265 0, when x > 0, E'(x) \u2264 0, so E(x) is decreasing; when x < 0, E'(x) \u2265 0, so E(x) is increasing. Therefore, the maximum of E(x) over all real numbers is at x = 0. Hence, E(x) \u2264 E(0) for all x. Thus, f(x)^2 \u2264 E(0) => |f(x)| \u2264 sqrt(E(0)), so f(x) is bounded.\n\nTherefore, this proves that f(x) is bounded. The critical step was constructing the energy function E(x) and analyzing its derivative based on the given ODE, leading to the conclusion that E(x) is bounded, hence f(x) is bounded.\n\nI think this should be the solution. Let me see if there are alternative approaches or potential gaps.\n\nAlternative approach: Suppose we consider the differential equation f''(x) + x g(x) f'(x) + f(x) = 0. If we can write this as a damped oscillator equation, where the damping term is x g(x) f'(x). Since damping usually dissipates energy, leading to the decay of oscillations. However, in this case, the damping coefficient is x g(x). So for x positive, damping is present (since x g(x) \u2265 0), which would cause energy to decrease. For x negative, the damping term is negative, which would act as an \"anti-damping\" or energy increase. However, in the energy analysis above, we saw that E(x) is decreasing for x > 0 and increasing for x < 0, but the maximum energy is at x = 0, so overall, energy is bounded by E(0).\n\nAnother point to consider: Could E(x) blow up as x approaches negative infinity? For x < 0, E'(x) \u2265 0, so E(x) is increasing as x decreases. However, if we take x approaching negative infinity, E(x) would approach some limit. But if E(x) is increasing as x approaches negative infinity, does it necessarily become unbounded? Not necessarily. If the integral of E'(x) from -infty to 0 is finite, then E(x) would be bounded. Let's compute the integral of E'(x) from -infty to 0:\n\nIntegral_{-infty}^0 E'(x) dx = E(0) - lim_{x->-infty} E(x). Since E'(x) = -2 x g(x) [f'(x)]^2. For x < 0, x is negative, so -2 x g(x) is positive, hence E'(x) is positive. Therefore, the integral from -infty to 0 of E'(x) dx = E(0) - E(-infty). But since E'(x) is positive for x < 0, E(-infty) must be less than or equal to E(0). Therefore, lim_{x->-infty} E(x) exists (since E(x) is non-decreasing and bounded above by E(0)) and is finite. Hence, E(x) is bounded as x approaches negative infinity. Similarly, as x approaches positive infinity, E(x) is non-increasing and bounded below (since E(x) is non-negative), so lim_{x->infty} E(x) exists and is finite. Therefore, E(x) is bounded everywhere, hence f(x) is bounded.\n\nTherefore, the key conclusion is that E(x) is bounded by E(0), which implies f(x) is bounded. The argument seems solid. I think this should be the proof required.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that \\( f(x) \\) is bounded under the given conditions, we analyze the energy function \\( E(x) = [f(x)]^2 + [f'(x)]^2 \\). \n\n**Step 1: Compute the derivative of \\( E(x) \\).**\n\\[\nE'(x) = 2f(x)f'(x) + 2f'(x)f''(x).\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( f''(x) = -f(x) - xg(x)f'(x) \\) from the given differential equation:\n\\[\nE'(x) = 2f(x)f'(x) + 2f'(x)\\left(-f(x) - xg(x)f'(x)\\right) = -2xg(x)[f'(x)]^2.\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Analyze the sign of \\( E'(x) \\).**\n- For \\( x > 0 \\), \\( xg(x) \\geq 0 \\) (since \\( g(x) \\geq 0 \\)), so \\( E'(x) \\leq 0 \\). Thus, \\( E(x) \\) is non-increasing for \\( x > 0 \\).\n- For \\( x < 0 \\), \\( xg(x) \\leq 0 \\), so \\( E'(x) \\geq 0 \\). Thus, \\( E(x) \\) is non-decreasing for \\( x < 0 \\).\n\n**Step 3: Establish boundedness of \\( E(x) \\).**\n- For \\( x \\geq 0 \\), \\( E(x) \\leq E(0) \\) because \\( E(x) \\) is non-increasing.\n- For \\( x \\leq 0 \\), \\( E(x) \\leq E(0) \\) because \\( E(x) \\) is non-decreasing as \\( x \\) approaches 0 from the left.\n\nThus, \\( E(x) \\leq E(0) \\) for all \\( x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\).\n\n**Step 4: Conclude boundedness of \\( f(x) \\).**\nSince \\( [f(x)]^2 \\leq E(x) \\leq E(0) \\), it follows that \\( |f(x)| \\leq \\sqrt{E(0)} \\). Therefore, \\( f(x) \\) is bounded by \\( \\sqrt{E(0)} \\), a constant determined by the initial values \\( f(0) \\) and \\( f'(0) \\).\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{f(x) \\text{ is bounded}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven that the function \\( f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R} \\) is twice-differentiable, and there exists a function \\( g : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R} \\) such that \\( g(x) \\geq 0 \\) and the equation \n\n\\[\nf(x) + f''(x) = -xg(x)f'(x)\n\\]\n\nholds for all \\( x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\), we are required to prove that \\( f(x) \\) is bounded.\n\n1. **Multiply both sides of the given differential equation by \\( f'(x) \\):**\n\n\\[\nf(x)f'(x) + f''(x)f'(x) = -xg(x) f'(x)^2\n\\]\n\n2. **Recognize that the left-hand side can be expressed as a derivative:**\n\n\\[\nf(x)f'(x) + f''(x)f'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\frac{f(x)^2}{2} \\right) + \\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\frac{f'(x)^2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n\\[\nf(x)f'(x) + f''(x)f'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\frac{f(x)^2}{2} + \\frac{f'(x)^2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\frac{f(x)^2}{2} + \\frac{f'(x)^2}{2} \\right) = -xg(x) f'(x)^2\n\\]\n\n3. **Note the behavior of the right-hand side:**\n\nSince \\( g(x) \\geq 0 \\) for all \\( x \\), the right-hand side \\( -xg(x) f'(x)^2 \\) is non-positive for \\( x > 0 \\) and non-negative for \\( x < 0 \\). This next step helps understand the significance of this:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\frac{f(x)^2}{2} + \\frac{f'(x)^2}{2} \\right) \\leq 0 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad x > 0\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\frac{f(x)^2}{2} + \\frac{f'(x)^2}{2} \\right) \\geq 0 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad x < 0\n\\]\n\n4. **Helper function analysis:**\n\nDefine \\( h(x) = \\frac{f(x)^2}{2} + \\frac{f'(x)^2}{2} \\). The function \\( h(x) \\) represents a combination of the square of \\( f \\) and \\( f' \\). From the above analysis, we observe:\n\n\\[\nh'(x) \\leq 0 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad x > 0\n\\]\n\\[\nh'(x) \\geq 0 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad x < 0\n\\]\n\nThis implies that \\( h(x) \\) is non-increasing for \\( x > 0 \\) and non-decreasing for \\( x < 0 \\).\n\n5. **Behavior at \\( x = 0 \\):**\n\nSince \\( h(x) \\) is non-increasing for positive \\( x \\) and non-decreasing for negative \\( x \\), it follows that \\( h(x) \\) has a maximum value at \\( x = 0 \\). Thus, \\( h(x) \\) is bounded above by \\( h(0) \\).\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n\nGiven that \\( h(x) = \\frac{f(x)^2}{2} + \\frac{f'(x)^2}{2} \\) and \\( h(x) \\) is bounded, both \\( f(x)^2 \\) and \\( f'(x)^2 \\) must be bounded. Hence, \\( f(x) \\) must be bounded.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(x) \\text{ is bounded}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "How many permutations of $a, b, c, d, e, f$ exist such that the letter $b$ always appears before the letter $e$?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out how many permutations of the letters a, b, c, d, e, f have b before e. Hmm, permutations usually involve arranging all the elements in different orders. Since there are 6 letters, the total number of permutations without any restrictions is 6 factorial, which is 720. But now we need to consider the condition where b comes before e. \n\nWait, so in any permutation, each letter can be in any position, but we want only those where b is to the left of e. How do I approach this? Maybe I can think about the positions of b and e relative to each other. In all possible permutations, b and e can be arranged in two ways: either b comes before e or e comes before b. Since these are the only two possibilities and all permutations are equally likely, maybe half of them have b before e and the other half have e before b. \n\nLet me test this idea with a smaller example. Suppose there are just two letters, b and e. The permutations are be and eb. So there's 2 permutations, and exactly half (1) have b before e. If we add another letter, say a, then the permutations are: a-be, a-eb, b-ae, b-ea, e-ab, e-ba, abe, aeb, bae, bea, eab, eba. Wait, that's 6 permutations. Let me check how many have b before e. \n\nLooking at each permutation:\n1. abe \u2013 b is before e.\n2. aeb \u2013 e is before b.\n3. bae \u2013 b before e.\n4. bea \u2013 b before e.\n5. eab \u2013 e before b.\n6. eba \u2013 e before b.\n\nSo out of 6 permutations, 3 have b before e. That's half. So even with an extra letter, the ratio is maintained. So maybe in general, for any permutation of the letters, the probability that b comes before e is 1/2. Therefore, if the total number is 6! = 720, then half of that would be 360. So the answer is 360 permutations.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure there's no other factors affecting this. For example, do other letters influence the position of b and e? Suppose we have more letters. If we fix the positions of b and e, the other letters can be arranged in any order. So for each arrangement of the other letters, b and e can be placed in the remaining spots either with b before e or e before b. Therefore, for each such arrangement, half will satisfy the condition. Hence, overall, the total number is 6! / 2 = 360.\n\nAlternatively, another way to think about it is: in the set of all permutations, the positions of b and e are equally likely to be in any order. Since there are two letters, the number of permutations where b precedes e is equal to the number where e precedes b. Therefore, they split the total permutations equally, so 720 / 2 = 360.\n\nYes, this seems to hold. So regardless of the number of other letters, as long as we are only concerned with the relative order of two specific letters, the number of permutations where one comes before the other is half the total number of permutations. Therefore, the answer should be 360.\n\nWait, but let me try another approach to confirm. Let's say we choose positions for b and e first. There are 6 positions, and we need to choose 2 distinct positions for b and e. The number of ways to choose 2 positions out of 6 is C(6,2) = 15. For each such choice, there's 2 ways to arrange b and e: either b in the first position and e in the second, or vice versa. Since we want only the cases where b is before e, that's half of these arrangements. So the number of favorable arrangements for b and e is C(6,2) = 15, and for each of these, the remaining 4 letters can be arranged in 4! ways. Therefore, total permutations would be 15 * 4! = 15 * 24 = 360. \n\nYes, that's the same answer as before. So this method also gives 360. Therefore, this confirms that the answer is indeed 360. \n\nAlternatively, another method: imagine building the permutation one letter at a time. The probability that b comes before e is 1/2, so multiplying by the total permutations gives 6! * 1/2 = 360. All these different reasoning paths lead to the same result, which makes me confident that 360 is the correct answer.", "deepseek_solution": "The number of permutations of \\(a, b, c, d, e, f\\) where \\(b\\) appears before \\(e\\) is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Total permutations without restrictions**:  \n   There are \\(6!\\) ways to arrange the six letters:  \n   \\[\n   6! = 720\n   \\]\n\n2. **Symmetry between \\(b\\) and \\(e\\)**:  \n   In any permutation, \\(b\\) either precedes \\(e\\) or follows \\(e\\). These two scenarios are equally likely. Thus, exactly half of all permutations satisfy \\(b\\) before \\(e\\):  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{6!}{2} = \\frac{720}{2} = 360\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\(\\boxed{360}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the set \\( \\{a, b, c, d, e, f\\} \\), we need to calculate the number of permutations in which the letter \\( b \\) appears before the letter \\( e \\).\n\n1. **All permutations**:\n   The total number of permutations of the set \\(\\{a, b, c, d, e, f\\}\\) is given by \\(6!\\) (since there are 6 elements):\n   \\[\n   6! = 720\n   \\]\n\n2. **Define subsets \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)**:\n   - Let \\( A \\) be the set of permutations where \\( b \\) appears before \\( e \\).\n   - Let \\( B \\) be the set of permutations where \\( b \\) appears after \\( e \\).\n\n   Clearly, \\( A \\cap B = \\varnothing \\) (disjoint sets) and \\( A \\cup B = I \\), where \\( I \\) is the set of all permutations. Therefore, \n   \\[\n   |A| + |B| = |I| = 720\n   \\]\n\n3. **Use mapping to show \\( |A| = |B| \\)**:\n   Define a mapping \\( f: A \\to B \\) such that for any permutation in \\( A \\) (\\(\\cdots b \\cdots e \\cdots \\)), map it to \\( B \\) by swapping \\( b \\) and \\( e \\). Thus, \\( f(\\cdots b \\cdots e \\cdots) = (\\cdots e \\cdots b \\cdots) \\).\n\n   This is a bijection because:\n   - It is injective (one-to-one): No two different permutations in \\( A \\) can map to the same permutation in \\( B \\).\n   - It is surjective (onto): Every permutation in \\( B \\) can be obtained by swapping \\( b \\) and \\( e \\) in some permutation in \\( A \\).\n\n   Therefore, \\( f \\) establishes a one-to-one correspondence between \\( A \\) and \\( B \\), so \\( |A| = |B| \\).\n\n4. **Calculate \\(|A|\\) and \\( |B| \\)**:\n   Since \\( |A| + |B| = 720 \\) and \\( |A| = |B| \\),\n   \\[\n   |A| = |B| = \\frac{720}{2} = 360\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   The number of permutations where \\(b\\) appears before \\(e\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{360}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A set of \\( 2n + 1 \\) points in the plane has no three collinear and no four concyclic. A circle is said to divide the set if it passes through 3 of the points and has exactly \\( n - 1 \\) points inside it. Show that the number of circles which divide the set is even if and only if \\( n \\) is even.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that the number of circles which divide the set is even if and only if n is even. The set has 2n+1 points with no three collinear and no four concyclic. A circle divides the set if it passes through 3 points and has exactly n-1 points inside it. Hmm.\n\nFirst, let me parse the problem. We have 2n+1 points, so an odd number. No three collinear means any three points can form a circle (since they aren't on a line). No four concyclic means that no four points lie on the same circle, so each circle can contain at most three points from the set.\n\nA circle that \"divides the set\" is one that passes through three points and has exactly n-1 points inside it. Since the total number of points is 2n+1, if a circle passes through three points and has n-1 inside, then there must be (2n +1) -