ency equal to the angle in the alternate segment, we have:\n   \n   \\[\n   \\angle BDF = \\angle BCD\n   \\]\n\n5. Consider $\\angle FDA = \\angle FAD$. Since $F$ is on the common tangent through $D$, both angles are equal due to the properties of the circle (angles subtended by the same arc).\n   \n6. Analyze the angle relationships established in prior steps and sum the angles involved:\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle ADC_1 = \\angle DAC + \\angle ACD\n   \\]\n   \n   Given that $\\angle FDA = \\angle FAD$ and $\\angle BDF = \\angle BCD$:\n   \n   \\[\n   \\angle ADC_1 = \\angle FDA + \\angle BDF\n   \\]\n\n7. Since $\\angle FDA$ and $\\angle BDF$ form part of the angle analysis centered at point $D$, we find:\n   \n   \\[\n   \\angle ADC_1 = \\angle ADB\n   \\]\n   \n   which shows the symmetry and hence equivalence of distances from point $A$ to lines $BD$ and $CD$.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nGiven the angle relationships and properties of tangents and inscribed angles, we have established that:\n\n\\(\n\\boxed{\\text{Point } A \\text{ is equidistant from the lines } BD \\text{ and } CD.}\n\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Points \\( C_{1} \\), \\( A_{1} \\), and \\( B_{1} \\) are taken on the sides \\( AB \\), \\( BC \\), and \\( CA \\) of an arbitrary triangle \\( ABC \\), respectively. Denote by \\( S_{1} \\), \\( S_{2} \\), and \\( S_{3} \\) the areas of triangles \\( AB_{1}C_{1} \\), \\( BA_{1}C_{1} \\), and \\( CA_{1}B_{1} \\), respectively. Prove that\n\\[\n\\sqrt{S_{1}}+\\sqrt{S_{2}}+\\sqrt{S_{3}} \\leq \\frac{3}{2} \\sqrt{S_{ABC}}\n\\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this geometry problem here. Let me read it again to make sure I understand. It says that in an arbitrary triangle ABC, points C1, A1, and B1 are taken on sides AB, BC, and CA respectively. Then S1, S2, S3 are the areas of triangles AB1C1, BA1C1, and CA1B1. I need to prove that the sum of the square roots of these areas is les