2 - y3) + x2(y3 - y1) + x3(y1 - y2)|.\n\nSo for triangle BA1C1:\n\nPoints B (1,0), A1 (1 - y, y), C1 (x, 0).\n\nPlugging into formula:\n\n\u00bd |1*(y - 0) + (1 - y)*(0 - 0) + x*(0 - y)|\n\n= \u00bd |1*y + (1 - y)*0 + x*(-y)|\n\n= \u00bd | y - x y | = \u00bd | y(1 - x) | = \u00bd y(1 - x). Since x and y are between 0 and 1, this is positive. So S2 = \u00bd y(1 - x).\n\nThird triangle, CA1B1: Points C (0,1), A1 (1 - y, y), B1 (0,1 - z).\n\nCompute its area. Using determinant formula again.\n\nPoints C (0,1), A1 (1 - y, y), B1 (0,1 - z).\n\nArea = \u00bd |0*(y - (1 - z)) + (1 - y)*((1 - z) - 1) + 0*(1 - y)|\n\nWait, let's compute term by term:\n\nx1=0, y1=1\n\nx2=1 - y, y2=y\n\nx3=0, y3=1 - z\n\nSo,\n\n\u00bd |0*(y - (1 - z)) + (1 - y)*((1 - z) - 1) + 0*(1 - y)|\n\n= \u00bd |0 + (1 - y)*(-z) + 0| = \u00bd | -z(1 - y) | = \u00bd z(1 - y). So S3 = \u00bd z(1 - y).\n\nTherefore, S1 = (1/2) x(1 - z), S2 = (1/2) y(1 - x), S3 = (1/2) z(1 - y). And the area of triangle ABC is 1/2 in this coordinate system.\n\nSo the original inequality becomes:\n\n\u221a[ (1/2) x(1 - z) ] + \u221a[ (1/2) y(1 - x) ] + \u221a[ (1/2) z(1 - y) ] \u2264 (3/2) \u221a(1/2)\n\nSimplify this:\n\nLeft side: Each term is \u221a(1/2) times \u221a[x(1 - z)], \u221a[y(1 - x)], \u221a[z(1 - y)]. So factoring out \u221a(1/2):\n\n\u221a(1/2) [ \u221a(x(1 - z)) + \u221a(y(1 - x)) + \u221a(z(1 - y)) ] \u2264 (3/2) \u221a(1/2)\n\nDividing both sides by \u221a(1/2):\n\n\u221a(x(1 - z)) + \u221a(y(1 - x)) + \u221a(z(1 - y)) \u2264 3/2\n\nSo the problem reduces to proving that \u221a(x(1 - z)) + \u221a(y(1 - x)) + \u221a(z(1 - y)) \u2264 3/2, where x, y, z are each in [0,1].\n\nWait, but in this coordinate system, the original triangle has area 1/2, so S_ABC = 1/2, hence \u221aS_ABC = \u221a(1/2). So the right-hand side of the original inequality is (3/2)\u221a(1/2), which matches after factoring out \u221a(1/2) from the left side. So this substitution seems valid.\n\nTherefore, the problem is equivalent to provin