oxed{p = r_{a} \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}\\)", "deepseek_solution": "To prove the given trigonometric identities related to a triangle, we start by understanding the relationships between the inradius \\(r\\), exradius \\(r_a\\), semiperimeter \\(p\\), and the half-angle trigonometric functions.\n\n### Part a)\n**Proof:**\n1. **Using cotangent half-angle formulas:**\n   \\[\n   \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = \\frac{p - b}{r} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{p - c}{r}\n   \\]\n   Adding these:\n   \\[\n   \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) + \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{(p - b) + (p - c)}{r} = \\frac{2p - b - c}{r} = \\frac{a}{r}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   a = r \\left( \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) + \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) \\right)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Using trigonometric identities:**\n   \\[\n   \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) + \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)} + \\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\n   \\]\n   Combining the fractions:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)} = \\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\n   \\]\n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{r \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\n   \\]\n\n### Part b)\n**Proof:**\n1. **Using tangent half-angle formulas:**\n   \\[\n   \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = \\frac{p - b}{r_a} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{p - c}{r_a}\n   \\]\n   Adding these:\n   \\[\n   \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) + \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{(p - b) + (p - c)}{r_a} = \\frac{2p - b - c}{r_a} = \\frac{a}{r_a}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   a = r_a \\left( \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) + \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) \\right)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Using trigonometric identities:**\n   \\[\n   \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) + \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)} + \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\n   \\]\n   Combining the fractions:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)} = \\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\n   \\]\n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{r_a \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\n   \\]\n\n### Part c)\n**Proof:**\n1. **Using cotangent half-angle formula:**\n   \\[\n   \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = \\frac{p - b}{r} \\implies p - b = r \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Using tangent half-angle formula:**\n   \\[\n   \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{p - b}{r_a} \\implies p - b = r_a \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n### Part d)\n**Proof:**\n**Using cotangent half-angle formula and exradius relation:**\n   \\[\n   \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) = \\frac{p}{r_a} \\implies p = r_a \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n### Final Answers\n\na) \\(\\boxed{a = r\\left(\\cot\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) + \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\\right) = \\frac{r \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}}\\)\n\nb) \\(\\boxed{a = r_{a}\\left(\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) + \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\\right) = \\frac{r_{a} \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}}\\)\n\nc) \\(\\boxed{p - b = r \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = r_{a} \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\)\n\nd) \\(\\boxed{p = r_{a} \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "#### Part (a)\n\n1. **Given** that \\( \\angle B = \\beta \\) and \\( \\angle C = \\gamma \\).\n\n2. **By definition of cotangent** for angles in a right triangle:\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{ctg}\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}\n   \\quad \\text{and} \\quad\n   \\operatorname{ctg}\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Summing these cotangents**:\n   \\[\n   a = r \\left(\\operatorname{ctg}\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) + \\operatorname{ctg}\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\\right) \n   = r \\left(\\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)} + \\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Finding the common denominator** and simplifying:\n   \\[\n   a = r \\left(\\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) + \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n5. **Using the sum-to-product identities**:\n   \\[\n   \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) + \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n6. **Since**:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha = \\pi - (\\beta + \\gamma)\n   \\]\n\n7. **We have**:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\alpha}{2} = \\frac{\\pi - (\\beta + \\gamma)}{2} = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Thus**:\n   \\[\n   \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi - (\\beta + \\gamma)}{2}\\right) = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n9. **Substituting** back:\n   \\[\n   a = r \\left(\\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n10. **And simplifying**:\n   \\[\n   a = r \\left(\\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi - \\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   = r \\left(\\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n11. **Therefore**:\n   \\[\n   a = r \\left(\\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   \\boxed{\\text{Part (a) is proven.}}\n   \\]\n\n#### Part (b)\n\n1. **Given** segments and angles** in a right triangle:\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{tg}\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}\n   \\quad \\text{and} \\quad\n   \\operatorname{tg}\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Summing these tangents**:\n   \\[\n   a = r_a \\left(\\operatorname{tg}\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) + \\operatorname{tg}\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\\right)\n   = r_a \\left(\\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)} + \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Finding the common denominator** and simplifying:\n   \\[\n   a = r_a \\left(\\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) + \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Using the sum-to-product identities**:\n   \\[\n   \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) + \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n5. **Substituting** back:\n   \\[\n   a = r_a \\left(\\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n6. **And simplifying**:\n   \\[\n   a = r_a \\left(\\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi - \\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   = r_a \\left(\\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n7. **Therefore**:\n   \\[\n   a = r_a \\left(\\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}\\right)\n   \\boxed{\\text{Part (b) is proven.}}\n   \\]\n\n#### Part (c)\n\n1. **Given** that \\( \\operatorname{ctg}(x) = \\frac{1}{\\tan(x)} \\):\n\n2. **We have**:\n   \\[\n   p - b = r \\operatorname{ctg}\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Given** the relation to the tangent**:\n   \\[\n   p - b = r_a \\operatorname{tg}\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Thus**:\n   \\[\n   r \\operatorname{ctg}\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = r_a \\operatorname{tg}\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n5. **Therefore**:\n   \\[\n   p - b = r \\operatorname{ctg}\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = r_a \\operatorname{tg}\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\n   \\boxed{\\text{Part (c) is proven.}}\n   \\]\n\n#### Part (d)\n\n1. **Given**:\n   \\[\n   p = r_a \\operatorname{ctg}\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Since when inscribed circle touches sides AB and AC in points P and Q respectively**:\n   \\[\n   p = AP = AQ = r_a \\operatorname{ctg}(\\frac{\\alpha}{2})\n   \\]\n\n3. **Thus**:\n   \\[\n   p = r_a \\operatorname{ctg}\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)\n   \\boxed{\\text{Part (d) is proven.}}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\(ABC\\) be a triangle such that \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ \\) and \\(\\angle B < \\angle C\\). The tangent at \\(A\\) to its circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) meets the line \\(BC\\) at \\(D\\). Let \\(E\\) be the reflection of \\(A\\) across \\(BC\\), \\(X\\) the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(BE\\), and \\(Y\\) the midpoint of \\(AX\\). Let the line \\(BY\\) meet \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(Z\\). Prove that the line \\(BD\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle \\(ADZ\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I need to prove that the line BD is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle ADZ. Let me start by understanding all the given information and constructing a clear diagram in my mind (or maybe sketch it out if I had paper). \n\nFirst, we have triangle ABC with a right angle at A, so ABC is a right-angled triangle at A. Also, angle B is less than angle C, meaning that side AC is longer than AB because in a right-angled triangle, the side opposite the larger angle is longer. So, AC > AB.\n\nThe tangent at A to the circumcircle \u03c9 of triangle ABC meets BC at D. Since ABC is right-angled at A, its circumcircle \u03c9 has BC as its diameter. Wait, is that correct? Because in a right-angled triangle, the circumcircle has the hypotenuse as its diameter. So yes, since angle A is 90\u00b0, BC must be the diameter of \u03c9. Therefore, the center of \u03c9 is the midpoint of BC. That's a key point.\n\nSo, the tangent at A to \u03c9. Since BC is the diameter, the tangent at A should be perpendicular to the radius at A. But the radius at A is from the center of