nSimilarly, let's consider triangle BMC. In triangle BMC, angle at M is 130\u00b0, angle at B is y (angle CBM), angle at C is p (angle BCM). So:\n\ny + p + 130\u00b0 = 180\u00b0\ny + p = 50\u00b0\nBut p + q = 70\u00b0, so q = 70\u00b0 - p. Therefore, q = 70\u00b0 - (50\u00b0 - y) = 20\u00b0 + y.\n\nNow, consider triangle AMC. In triangle AMC, angle at M is 120\u00b0, angle at A is n (angle CAM), angle at C is q (angle ACM). So:\n\nn + q + 120\u00b0 = 180\u00b0\nn + q = 60\u00b0\nBut n = 50\u00b0 - m, and q = 20\u00b0 + y. So,\n\n(50\u00b0 - m) + (20\u00b0 + y) = 60\u00b0\n70\u00b0 - m + y = 60\u00b0\n- m + y = -10\u00b0\nBut from earlier, m = y + 10\u00b0, so substituting:\n\n- (y + 10\u00b0) + y = -10\u00b0\n- y - 10\u00b0 + y = -10\u00b0\n-10\u00b0 = -10\u00b0\nHmm, this simplifies to an identity, which doesn't give new information. So, we need another equation.\n\nAt this point, trigonometric Ceva's Theorem might be necessary. Let's recall the formula again:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin \\angle BAM}{\\sin \\angle CAM} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin \\angle ACM}{\\sin \\angle BCM} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin \\angle CBM}{\\sin \\angle ABM} = 1\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known angles:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin m}{\\sin n} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin q}{\\sin p} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin y}{\\sin x} = 1\n\\]\n\nWe have:\n\nm = y + 10\u00b0\n\nn = 50\u00b0 - m = 50\u00b0 - (y + 10\u00b0) = 40\u00b0 - y\n\nq = 20\u00b0 + y\n\np = 50\u00b0 - y\n\nx = 60\u00b0 - y\n\nSo, substituting all in terms of y:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin(y + 10\u00b0)}{\\sin(40\u00b0 - y)} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin(20\u00b0 + y)}{\\sin(50\u00b0 - y)} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin y}{\\sin(60\u00b0 - y)} = 1\n\\]\n\nSo, we need to solve for y in the equation above. This seems complicated, but maybe we can plug in possible values for y and see if the equation holds. Since y is an angle inside triangle ABC at vertex B, which is 60\u00b0, and M is inside the triangle, y must be between 0\u00b0 and 60\u00b0. Let's see if we can find such a y.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps 