eft(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right) \\).\n   - After substituting \\( t = x + \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\), the equation simplifies to checking critical points. However, solving the resulting quintic equation shows no roots in the interval, indicating no critical points inside the interval.\n\n3. **Evaluating Endpoints**:\n   - At \\( x = -\\frac{5}{12}\\pi \\):\n     - \\( \\tan \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1 \\)\n     - \\( \\tan \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = -1 \\)\n     - \\( \\cos \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\)\n     - \\( y = 1 - (-1) + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 2 + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\approx 2.7071 \\)\n   - At \\( x = -\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\):\n     - \\( \\tan \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sqrt{3} \\)\n     - \\( \\tan \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\)\n     - \\( \\cos \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\)\n     - \\( y = \\sqrt{3} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{11}{6}\\sqrt{3} \\approx 3.175 \\)\n\nThe maximum value occurs at \\( x = -\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\) with the exact value \\( \\frac{11}{6}\\sqrt{3} \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{11\\sqrt{3}}{6}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Observe the expression:**\n   Given the function: \n   \\[\n   y = \\tan \\left(x + \\frac{2}{3} \\pi\\right) - \\tan \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + \\cos \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n   \\]\n   The difference in angles within the tangent functions:\n   \\[\n   \\left(x + \\frac{2}{3} \\pi\\right) - \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n   \\]\n   This observation is crucial for simplifying the terms.\n\n2. **Simplify using trigonometric identities:**\n   Utilize the identity for tangent:\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\left(\\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -\\cot \\theta\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\left(x + \\frac{2}{3} \\pi\\right) = \\tan \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -\\cot \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substitute variables and trigonometric functions:**\n   Substituting into the expression for \\( y \\):\n   \\[\n   y = -\\cot \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) - \\tan \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + \\cos \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n   \\]\n   \n   Rewrite the expression:\n   \\[\n   y = - \\left(\\cot \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + \\tan \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right) + \\cos \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Further simplification using trigonometric identities:**\n   Utilize the identity \\( \\tan \\theta + \\cot \\theta = \\frac{2}{\\sin 2\\theta} \\):\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{2}{\\sin \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)} + \\cos \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n5. **Determine the intervals:**\n   Given \\( x \\in \\left[-\\frac{5}{12} \\pi, -\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right] \\),\n   \\[\n   x + \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, -\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right]\n   \\]\n   Then,\n   \\[\n   2x + \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, -\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right]\n   \\]\n\n6. **Analyze monotonicity:**\n   The function \\( -\\frac{2}{\\sin \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)} \\) and \\( \\cos \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\) are both monotonic increasing within this interval.\n\n7. **Evaluate at the boundary of the interval:**\n   At \\( x = -\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\):\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{2}{\\sin \\left(2 \\cdot -\\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)} + \\cos \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Simplify the arguments:\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot -\\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\pi}{3}\n   \\]\n   and\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{\\pi}{6} = -\\frac{\\pi}{6}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Calculate trigonometric values:**\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\cos \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Final computation:**\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{2}{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{8}{2\\sqrt{3}} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n   \\]\n   Simplify to a common denominator:\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\cdot 2 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{6} + \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{6} = \\frac{11\\sqrt{3}}{6}\n   \\]\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{11\\sqrt{3}}{6}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In some 16 cells of an $8 \\times 8$ board, rooks are placed. What is the minimum number of pairs of rooks that can attack each other in this configuration?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out the minimum number of pairs of rooks that can attack each other when 16 rooks are placed on an 8x8 chessboard. Let me start by recalling how rooks attack each other. A rook can move any number of squares vertically or horizontally, so two rooks attack each other if they are in the same row or the same column. Each pair of rooks in the same row contributes one attacking pair, and similarly for columns. But wait, actually, if there are multiple rooks in a row or column, the number of attacking pairs is the combination of the number of rooks taken two at a time. For example, if there are k rooks in a row, then the number of attacking pairs in that row is C(k, 2) = k(k-1)/2. The same goes for columns. So the total number of attacking pairs is the sum over all rows of C(k_i, 2) plus the sum over all columns of C(m_j, 2), where k_i is the number of rooks in row i and m_j is the number in column j.\n\nBut we need to minimize this total. Since there are 16 rooks on an 8x8 board, we have to distribute them in such a way that the number of attacking pairs is as small as possible. To minimize the number of attacking pairs, we should try to spread out the rooks as 